 "title": "Future of dar, me doy cuenta, le / les = to him, to her, to them",
 "cards": [
  {
   "title": "dar in the future",
   "body": "'Will you give it to me?' here is a literal question about the future, not a polite request (for that, use 'can you').",
   "examples": [
    {
     "en": "I will give",
     "es": "daré"
    },
    {
     "en": "Will you guys give it to me?",
     "es": "¿Me lo darán?"
    }
   ],
   "t": 0
  },
  {
   "title": "First: who is doing the verb?",
   "body": "",
   "examples": [
    {
     "en": "They'll give you something.",
     "es": "Te darán algo."
    },
    {
     "en": "They won't give it to us.",
     "es": "No nos lo darán."
    }
   ],
   "t": 100.8
  },
  {
   "title": "darse cuenta = to realise",
   "body": "'I give myself account.' Realizar means to carry out a project. ¿Te das cuenta? is a very common 'you know what I mean?'.",
   "examples": [
    {
     "en": "I realise.",
     "es": "Me doy cuenta."
    },
    {
     "en": "Do you realise? / You know?",
     "es": "¿Te das cuenta?"
    },
    {
     "en": "We realise.",
     "es": "Nos damos cuenta."
    }
   ],
   "t": 178.7
  },
  {
   "title": "le / les = to him, her, them",
   "body": "Me, te, nos don't change for 'to/for'. But lo/la become le, and los/las become les, when the meaning is 'to/for' the person.",
   "examples": [
    {
     "en": "I give it.",
     "es": "Lo doy."
    },
    {
     "en": "I give (to) them.",
     "es": "Les doy."
    },
    {
     "en": "I'm giving them something.",
     "es": "Les doy algo."
    }
   ],
   "t": 275.1
  },
  {
   "title": "le with haber",
   "body": "Le can be to him, to her or to formal you.",
   "examples": [
    {
     "en": "We've spoken to him / her.",
     "es": "Le hemos hablado."
    },
    {
     "en": "They've talked about it.",
     "es": "Lo han hablado."
    },
    {
     "en": "They've spoken to them.",
     "es": "Les han hablado."
    }
   ],
   "t": 417.7
  },
  {
   "title": "Feel it: direct or indirect?",
   "body": "Ignore whether English uses 'to' (you listen TO him but hear him). Ask: is the person cooked, or cooked for?",
   "examples": [
    {
     "en": "He's cooking something for me / us / you.",
     "es": "Me / Nos / Te cocina algo."
    },
    {
     "en": "He's cooking him something.",
     "es": "Le cocina algo."
    },
    {
     "en": "He's cooking them (the tarts).",
     "es": "Las cocina."
    },
    {
     "en": "He's cooking them for us.",
     "es": "Nos las cocina."
    }
   ],
   "t": 606.5
